@Paul @ UK. Tin Tin notwithstanding and I remember standing in an UWS bookstore reading Art Spiegelman's Maus (when he was still unknown and before he won the Pulitzer (was it?) from cover to cover and couldn't put it down - it was that hypnotizing - but it still was narrative based. But Lichtenstein was aiming at something altogether different - basically saying something (in the vein of what High Art does): that which is unutterable. If it could be translated and brought down to the quotidian it would lose the X factor that makes it High Art as opposed to a folk art. May be a case of apples and oranges cuz when I was hanging out at MOMA as a teen and visual art was my religion (it was our candy store to hang out in after the day was over at Stuyvesant H.S. - and maybe why I'm so protective of it's elite body of students who could support each other and which did not happen in a neighborhood High Schools). A pox on Mamdani for trying yet again like other mayors to crush that special place. But at the same time I was transfixed by the underground art of Crumb and later Spiegelman.
Artistic Director 
The Great American Play Series

@Joseph Carmine MagnottiTampa, FL  The truth of what you're saying is clear but it was because it was the beginning of 'the Fifth Estate" (the media) creeping its way to power. As a young kid I remember discussing the war in class before it was deemed 'unwinnable'. And Walter Conkrite's announcement after Tet. Fact is, we never lost a battle & the politicians & young utopians (like myself) were propagandized to be anti-military. Hard to pick up a gun after being enraptured by the Beatles singing 'All you need is Love'. The zeitgeist was flying on the wealth of our WWII victory & the standard of living we had reached made the struggle in Vietnam seem very irrelevant to the 'go go Sixties'. But if you read the SF sagas that are now coming out you'll see how highly efficient & brave those warriors were. We just didn't have a warrior culture alive & well at the time. (truthful disclosure) I left the country & ended up in the SE war zone to see for myself what the situation was which is where I came to understand that each country's citizens were chauvinistic - even in Herat - which showed later - so that there were no Valhallas out there. Even now, when there are three potential nuclear confrontations (esp from N.Korea) existing, we are more concerned with identity issues like men deciding they're women etc. Or that the 1619 project was the basis of the country. (IMHO ridiculous: as Washington, Adams, Jefferson led the way) Beware cynicism.
Artistic Dir
Grt American Play Series
